1
00:00:00,299 --> 00:00:04,959
The coin disappears, the number changes, and the bar fills a
little further.

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:09,062
This is our real pickup counter, shown with a native interface
control.

3
00:00:09,188 --> 00:00:14,180
We'll build it on the five-coin woodland, then check the display
from an empty bar to a full one.

4
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:24,283
We'll choose the control, connect its current value and range, add
it to a layout,

5
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:30,143
and make sure the player's class uses that layout. Then we'll
collect the coins and retry. A bar

6
00:00:30,168 --> 00:00:34,928
that looks right in the editing preview is only the beginning; we
need to see it respond during play.

7
00:00:35,728 --> 00:00:40,957
Use a separate copy of the five-coin lesson save. Keep the tested
version from last time available.

8
00:00:41,076 --> 00:00:45,090
It already has the pickup graph and the predefined Player variable
Coins.

9
00:00:45,286 --> 00:00:48,858
The first library-entry example uses a separate setup copy;

10
00:00:48,912 --> 00:00:52,753
we'll configure and test the complete lesson HUD in our working
copy.

11
00:00:53,437 --> 00:00:59,496
Open the System Menu and choose Manage UI Control Groups. In UI
Control Group Library,

12
00:00:59,521 --> 00:01:05,589
use Add Server Control and choose Progress Bar. The library is
where we make reusable controls.

13
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:11,495
We'll call the lesson control CoinProgress so it's easy to find
later. Your own recognisable name is fine.

14
00:01:18,456 --> 00:01:22,838
There are three places to keep track of. The library holds our
control template.

15
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:26,763
The interface layout decides which controls appear and where they
sit.

16
00:01:26,903 --> 00:01:30,825
The player's class selects the layout used in play. We'll go
through each one.

17
00:01:30,887 --> 00:01:34,352
Saving a template alone doesn't put a new control on the player's
screen.

18
00:01:35,114 --> 00:01:38,089
Select the progress bar and open its Functions settings.

19
00:01:38,229 --> 00:01:43,304
We'll use Horizontal, filling from left to right. The colour and
size can suit your scene.

20
00:01:43,412 --> 00:01:48,080
For this exercise, concentrate on the data settings: Current
Progress Value,

21
00:01:48,105 --> 00:01:53,248
Minimum Value, and Maximum Value. Those three fields determine
what the fill means.

22
00:01:54,137 --> 00:02:00,677
Open the Current Progress Value selector. In Variable Library,
expand Player Themself and select Coins.

23
00:02:00,945 --> 00:02:06,513
That is the Player variable updated by the pickup graph. We didn't
store our total on the disappearing coin.

24
00:02:06,644 --> 00:02:11,478
Keeping the data owner consistent is what lets this display follow
the correct counter.

25
00:02:12,256 --> 00:02:18,571
Set Progress Bar Style to one-to-one scale. This native style
shows the current amount against the goal.

26
00:02:23,128 --> 00:02:29,132
In our version, it includes one decimal place, such as zero point
zero out of five point zero.

27
00:02:29,229 --> 00:02:34,848
That's a display format. Keep the Coins variable as Integer,
because our graph still counts whole coins.

28
00:02:35,675 --> 00:02:37,733
Notice Progress Preview in the editor.

29
00:02:37,851 --> 00:02:41,583
Its example value helps you see how the control looks while
designing it.

30
00:02:41,626 --> 00:02:46,734
It doesn't mean the player has collected that many coins. Our
actual test should begin at zero.

31
00:02:46,831 --> 00:02:50,866
We'll use the live display, rather than the preview setting, to
check the binding.

32
00:02:51,584 --> 00:02:57,072
Use Save as Template and choose New Template. Name it CoinProgress
and confirm.

33
00:02:57,233 --> 00:02:59,952
This creates the reusable item in the library.

34
00:03:00,049 --> 00:03:06,275
We'll reopen its settings to finish the minimum and maximum
bindings. When updating an existing template,

35
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:10,775
use Save Changes, rather than making another new template with a
similar name.

36
00:03:11,609 --> 00:03:17,026
For the range, we'll use two more predefined standard variables on
the Default Player template.

37
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:22,305
Open Combat Preset, Player, Player Template, Default, and Edit
Player.

38
00:03:22,424 --> 00:03:26,665
In Custom Variable, add ProgressMin as Integer with a default of
zero,

39
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:31,270
and CoinGoal as Integer with a default of five. Save those
definitions.

40
00:03:50,585 --> 00:03:56,055
Return to CoinProgress. For Minimum Value, select Player Themself
and ProgressMin.

41
00:03:56,089 --> 00:03:59,767
For Maximum Value, select Player Themself and CoinGoal.

42
00:04:00,014 --> 00:04:04,257
Zero is our empty state; five is the goal for the five coins we've
placed.

43
00:04:04,344 --> 00:04:09,245
We can also use that same goal in the challenge rule later, so the
display and rule agree.

44
00:04:23,609 --> 00:04:29,387
With this range, one coin fills one fifth of the bar, and three
coins fill three fifths.

45
00:04:29,412 --> 00:04:35,256
Five reaches the maximum. The bar is showing the current value
between its minimum and maximum.

46
00:04:35,396 --> 00:04:40,089
If you change the number of coins in your own challenge, review
the goal and the rule together.

47
00:04:40,771 --> 00:04:43,736
Choose a size and position that you can read while moving.

48
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:49,187
I keep the bar near the upper centre, with room around the
original exit and direction display.

49
00:04:49,282 --> 00:04:52,812
Check the number against both empty and filled backgrounds.

50
00:04:52,866 --> 00:04:59,397
A wide bar won't help if its text disappears into the landscape.
You can choose different colours and spacing.

51
00:05:00,189 --> 00:05:06,769
After setting the range, use Save Changes. In Save Template
Changes, choose Confirm Changes.

52
00:05:06,802 --> 00:05:12,197
The dialogue shows no existing references here, because we haven't
added the template to a layout yet. Check

53
00:05:12,222 --> 00:05:19,560
all three bindings: Coins, ProgressMin, and CoinGoal, each from
Player Themself. Then continue to the layout.

54
00:05:20,195 --> 00:05:26,058
Open Interface Layout and choose Default Layout. Use Add UI
control, open Custom Template,

55
00:05:26,083 --> 00:05:30,884
and select CoinProgress. Arrange the instance in the PC layout and
save.

56
00:05:31,003 --> 00:05:35,663
The library template supplies the control; this layout instance
puts it on the screen.

57
00:05:35,824 --> 00:05:39,594
Keep the original game controls visible and usable while placing
yours.

58
00:05:43,285 --> 00:05:48,969
In Combat Preset, open Player, then Class, and select the class
used by your player.

59
00:05:49,045 --> 00:05:51,977
In this lesson, it's the existing Custom Class.

60
00:05:52,075 --> 00:05:57,253
Check Select Layout and choose the layout you edited. Our readback
is Default Layout.

61
00:05:57,335 --> 00:06:01,981
This final connection tells the player's class which arrangement
to display during the challenge.

62
00:06:16,308 --> 00:06:19,389
Quick Save the scene and wait for the saved acknowledgement.

63
00:06:19,465 --> 00:06:23,050
Start a fresh private Test Play and wait for the character and
HUD.

64
00:06:23,117 --> 00:06:26,348
We haven't added a countdown or a completion rule yet.

65
00:06:26,530 --> 00:06:31,759
This test is about the coin counter, its fill, and the layout
actually used by the player.

66
00:06:46,294 --> 00:06:52,067
At the start, the bar reads zero point zero out of five point
zero, with no green fill.

67
00:06:52,146 --> 00:06:55,782
That's the expected empty state for this version of the native
control.

68
00:06:55,943 --> 00:07:01,777
If yours begins full, check the minimum, maximum, and source
variable before collecting anything.

69
00:07:02,002 --> 00:07:04,889
The existing coins should still be waiting along the trail.

70
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:11,679
Walk into the first coin and stop. The coin disappears, and the
display changes to one out of five,

71
00:07:11,734 --> 00:07:16,508
with about twenty percent fill. We haven't added a second counting
graph for the interface.

72
00:07:16,698 --> 00:07:21,102
The pickup graph changes Coins, and this control reads that same
value.

73
00:07:21,348 --> 00:07:24,892
That connection keeps the number and the visual fill together.

74
00:07:25,689 --> 00:07:32,589
Continue along the route. Two coins show about forty percent,
three show sixty, and four show eighty.

75
00:07:32,644 --> 00:07:37,325
After the fifth, the display reaches five out of five and the bar
is full.

76
00:07:37,422 --> 00:07:43,056
Check the middle values as well as the final one. A
correct-looking full bar could hide an incorrect range.

77
00:07:58,590 --> 00:08:04,616
Choose Retry Challenge. The coins return, and the HUD goes back to
zero out of five with an empty bar.

78
00:08:04,692 --> 00:08:09,390
We haven't manually filled or cleared the control for this check.
The new challenge instance

79
00:08:09,415 --> 00:08:14,359
starts with the predefined Player values again, and the native
binding displays the new counter.

80
00:08:17,632 --> 00:08:20,436
If you can't see your control, check the chain in order.

81
00:08:20,534 --> 00:08:23,870
Is the template saved? Is its instance in the layout?

82
00:08:23,967 --> 00:08:28,755
Does the active class select that layout? Then check its
visibility and position.

83
00:08:29,002 --> 00:08:33,142
These checks answer a different question from whether the pickup
graph is counting correctly.

84
00:08:33,905 --> 00:08:37,882
If the control appears but its value is wrong, return to the
bindings.

85
00:08:38,001 --> 00:08:43,066
Check Player Themself, the exact variable name Coins, and the
zero-to-five range.

86
00:08:43,249 --> 00:08:47,365
The graph's target and the UI's source need to describe the same
Player data.

87
00:08:47,537 --> 00:08:51,699
You can compare the native log with the HUD to narrow down which
part needs attention.

88
00:08:52,418 --> 00:08:59,388
Try one pickup, a middle value, a full bar, and a retry in your
own copy. Explain which setting supplies

89
00:08:59,413 --> 00:09:05,698
the current value, which two settings define the range, and why
the layout and class are both involved.

90
00:09:05,906 --> 00:09:11,604
The purpose is a readable display that follows real data, rather
than a copy of my screen coordinates.

91
00:09:12,318 --> 00:09:17,438
Keep the working HUD lesson save. Next, we'll add a native
two-minute countdown,

92
00:09:17,463 --> 00:09:20,907
a deliberate start, and a result when time runs out.

93
00:09:21,090 --> 00:09:27,142
We'll use the tested Player counter and goal to decide whether the
challenge ends in success or failure.
